Thanks for the great video. Some key observations that helped me understand better - 

1. For every collision that occurs, the car that's ahead will always have slower speed than the car behind and the car that's behind will have it's speed reduced down when the collision occurs. I think this is kinda obvious since the car that was behind started at an initial position far behind the car that was ahead and was still able to catch up to it means it had head greater speed to begin with.

2. Why exactly do we pop the blue car from the stack when we know it collides with green? What if the the orange car collided with the blue car even before the blue car could collide with the green car? 
If that was the case, the orange car would collide with the blue car and have it's speed reduced down to the blue car's speed (using 1.), and we already know that the blue car collides with the green car, so the orange car would also collide with the green car. Hence, we don't need to check if the orange car collides with the blue car specifically, we just need to check if it collides with the 'leader' of the fleet ahead which is the green car.

class Solution:
    def carFleet(self, target: int, position: List[int], speed: List[int]) -> int:
        cars = [(p, s) for p,s in zip(position, speed)]
        cars.sort()
        stack = [cars[-1]]
        for i in range(len(cars) - 2, -1, -1):
            if not ((target - cars[i][0]) / cars[i][1] <= (target - stack[-1][0]) / stack[-1][1]):
                stack.append(cars[i])
        return len(stack)

